<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Brown Russwurm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1799">1799</a>–<a href="/years/1851">1851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Brown Russwurm was a Jamaican-born American abolitionist and one of the first African American college graduates, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bowdoin-college">Bowdoin College</a> in 1826. He co-founded Freedom's Journal, the first African American newspaper, in 1827. After emigrating to Liberia, he became in 1836 the first Black governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> colony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-palmas">Cape Palmas</a>, serving until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-brown-russwurm.jpg" alt="John Brown Russwurm" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified Artist · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Russwurm_Smithsonian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
